--TEST--
`Core\Uri::buildQuery` and `parseQuery` are inverse over a sweep of query strings — the array survives, the text is a fixed point, and key order is insertion order — with the two keys the wire cannot spell: one holding the bracket convention's own characters, and one that is empty — spec § 12
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The neighbouring case shows what one call writes, row by row. This one asks
// the question a row cannot: is the pair actually inverse. Two properties, both
// counted over the whole table so a member answering plausibly on any single
// row still fails here.
//
//   stable      — `parseQuery(buildQuery($a))` is `$a` again, for the array
//                 `parseQuery` itself produced. This is the direction that
//                 matters: a query string has several spellings for one set of
//                 parameters, so the array is what has to survive.
//   idempotent  — and once written, the text is a fixed point, so a value that
//                 makes a second trip is not escaped a second time.
array<string> $queries = [
    "a=1&b=2",
    "a[]=1&a[]=2",
    "a[b][c]=d&a[b][e][]=f",
    "x=&y",
    "a+b=c+d",
    "n[0]=z&n[1]=y",
    "a[b][c][d][e]=x",
    "",
];
// `parseQuery` answers a value as `bytes` (spec § 12) and `Core\Json::encode`
// has no encoding for one, so every comparison of two whole arrays below runs
// through this walk. It converts leaves and nothing else, so the nesting and
// the key order — which are what is being compared — are untouched.
class Shape {
    public static function of(string $query): tainted string {
        return Core\Json::encode(Shape::textual(Core\Uri::parseQuery($query)));
    }

    public static function textual(array<mixed> $values): array<mixed> {
        array<mixed> $out = [];
        foreach ($values as tainted string $key => mixed $value) {
            ?array<mixed> $nested = $value as ?array<mixed>;
            if ($nested == null) {
                $out[$key] = $value as bytes as string;
            } else {
                $out[$key] = Shape::textual($nested);
            }
        }
        return $out;
    }
}

int $rows = 0;
int $stable = 0;
int $idempotent = 0;
foreach ($queries as string $q) {
    $rows = $rows + 1;
    var $once = Core\Uri::parseQuery($q);
    var $text = Core\Uri::buildQuery($once);
    // `Core\Json::encode` is how two `array<mixed>` are compared as a whole:
    // it renders the nesting and the key order in one string, where reading
    // the arrays element by element would stop at the first level.
    if (Shape::of($text) == Core\Json::encode(Shape::textual($once))) {
        $stable = $stable + 1;
    }
    if (Core\Uri::buildQuery(Core\Uri::parseQuery($text)) == $text) {
        $idempotent = $idempotent + 1;
    }
}
echo "rows ", $rows, " stable ", $stable, " idempotent ", $idempotent, "\n";

// Key order is the array's own order, in both directions — `buildQuery` never
// sorts, and `parseQuery` records the pairs as it reads them. A query string
// is a sequence, so a member that ordered by name would change what a signed
// request signs.
echo Core\Uri::buildQuery(["b" => "1", "a" => "2", "c" => "3"]), "\n";
echo Core\Uri::buildQuery(Core\Uri::parseQuery("z=1&m=2&a=3")), "\n";

// The bound the sweep above is written to stay inside: the inverse holds for
// every array `parseQuery` produced, and not for every array. A key holding
// the bracket convention's own characters is escaped like any other byte — so
// the text is right, and there is no second spelling that would let the
// reader tell it from the nested value it is now indistinguishable from. PHP's
// `http_build_query`/`parse_str` pair loses the same key the same way; the
// wire has one bracket, not two.
echo Core\Uri::buildQuery(["a[b]" => "v"]), "\n";
echo Shape::of(Core\Uri::buildQuery(["a[b]" => "v"])), "\n";

// The other one: an empty name is written — the pair is a `=` with nothing to
// its left — and then dropped on the way back, because a pair whose name
// decodes to nothing has no name to record it under. Written and dropped, not
// refused: `buildQuery` answers text a reader accepts, and the reader is where
// the pair goes.
echo "[", Core\Uri::buildQuery(["" => "v"]), "]\n";
echo Shape::of(Core\Uri::buildQuery(["" => "v"])), "\n";

// An empty *value* is not the empty case: it keeps its pair, at every depth,
// which is what makes the `x=&y` row above round-trip rather than shrink.
echo Core\Uri::buildQuery(["a" => ["", "x"]]), "\n";
echo "[", Core\Uri::buildQuery([]), "]\n";
--EXPECT--
rows 8 stable 8 idempotent 8
b=1&a=2&c=3
z=1&m=2&a=3
a%5Bb%5D=v
{"a":{"b":"v"}}
[=v]
[]
a%5B0%5D=&a%5B1%5D=x
[]
